# 0005. One server replica owns scheduling and state; CLI and Telegram use the REST API

- Status: Accepted (temporary, technical debt)
- Date: 2026-11-11

Accepted by the owner on 2026-11-11 as a **temporary** decision, kept because there is no
time to build an alternative now, not because it is the target design: one server process
holds all credentials and state; every other client goes through its REST API. Revisit when
availability matters or before running more than one server; the replacement gets a new ADR
that supersedes this one.

## Technical details
- **Problem**: the scheduler, startup reconciler and in-flight request counts live in one
  process, and several entry points (HTTP, CLI, Telegram) need the same operations.
- **Server**: `cmd/server` is the only process with the database URL, RunPod key, HF token
  and runtime key secret. `deployments/kubernetes/base/server.yaml` runs `replicas: 1` with
  `strategy: Recreate`, so two servers never overlap; non-root, read-only root filesystem.
- **In-memory state**: `deployment.Activity` (`core/domain/deployment/activity.go`) counts
  in-flight requests per deployment (`Begin(id)` returns the `end` func); the scheduler's
  `Tick` health-checks serving deployments, stops idle ones (`StopIfIdle` re-checks idleness
  at stop time) and retries cleanup. Both assume they are the only instance.
- **Clients**: `cmd/cli` and `cmd/telegram` are REST clients through `internal/apiclient`,
  sharing wire types in `internal/apitypes`; archtest keeps both free of core and adapter
  imports ([0001](0001-hexagonal-core-with-enforced-boundaries.adr)). The bot holds only its
  Telegram token and an API token.
- **Network**: NetworkPolicies let the bot reach the server, and the server reach PostgreSQL
  and RunPod (to be verified after the first deploy, SEC-006).
- **Consequences**: credentials sit in one pod; the bot's blast radius is small; activity
  tracking is simple and correct. No high availability; a server restart drops in-flight
  streams.
- **Technical debt**: scaling out needs shared state (e.g. in-flight counts in PostgreSQL)
  and leader election for the scheduler and reconciler before a second replica can run.
  Never scale out without a superseding ADR.

## Technologies and alternatives
- Involved: Kubernetes (Deployment, NetworkPolicy, kustomize overlays).
- Alternatives: Telegram bot calling the core directly: a second process with provider
  credentials racing the scheduler; leader election for several replicas: complexity a
  personal platform doesn't need yet.
